            "@type": "FAQPage",
            "mainEntity": [
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What is a dividend reinvestment plan (DRIP)?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "A DRIP automatically uses dividend payments to purchase additional shares of the same stock or fund rather than distributing cash. Most brokerages and many companies offer DRIPs. Over time, the reinvested shares generate their own dividends, creating a compounding loop that accelerates growth."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Are reinvested dividends taxable?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Yes. In a taxable brokerage account, dividends are taxable in the year you receive them — even if they are immediately reinvested. Qualified dividends are taxed at the lower capital gains rate (0%, 15%, or 20% depending on income); ordinary dividends are taxed as income. This annual tax drag is why tax-advantaged accounts like Roth IRAs are the ideal DRIP wrapper."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "How much does reinvesting dividends add to long-term returns?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Historically, the difference is substantial. Federal Reserve FRED data and Robert Shiller's S&P 500 dataset show that price-only returns have averaged roughly 4% per year while total return (dividends reinvested) averages closer to 7% — nearly doubling the compounding rate. Over 20–30 years, that gap produces dramatically different ending balances."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Should I reinvest dividends or take the cash?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "For long-term investors who do not need current income, reinvesting almost always builds more wealth, since compounding multiplies the effect over time. For retirees or income-focused investors who need cash flow, taking dividends as income makes sense. The right choice depends on your financial phase and tax situation."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What return rate should I use for a DRIP calculation?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Use a total-return rate that blends price appreciation and dividend yield. For a broad U.S. equity index, a 7–10% historical total return is commonly cited. Using a price-only rate (around 4% for the S&P 500) would understate DRIP's impact. Check current data on Federal Reserve FRED for up-to-date figures."
                }
              }
            ]
